Close the input file after q with -i.
With -i each file is processed on its own, and q ends only the current
file. But q left that file open, at the line after the last one read.
On Windows, where an open file cannot be replaced, "sed -i 2q f" then
failed with "can't move" and left f unchanged whenever f had more than
two lines. Elsewhere the rename succeeded, but the next file was not
read from its start: the following process_files() went on reading the
rest of the previous file, so "sed -i 1q a b", with three lines in a,
replaced b with the third line of a. Close the file after q and move on
to the next one.

---
 editors/sed.c |    7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

--- a/editors/sed.c
+++ b/editors/sed.c
@@ -1791,6 +1791,13 @@
 			fchown(nonstdoutfd, statbuf.st_uid, statbuf.st_gid);
 
 			process_files();
+			/* After 'q' the file is still open: close it, so that
+			 * it can be replaced and the next file is read */
+			if (G.current_fp) {
+				fclose(G.current_fp);
+				G.current_fp = NULL;
+				G.current_input_file++;
+			}
 			fflush(G.nonstdout);
 			if (ferror(G.nonstdout)) {
 				xfunc_error_retval = 4;  /* It's what gnu sed exits with... */
